===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|weder}}, {{m|enm|wedir}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weder}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wedr}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wedrą}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*wedʰrom}} (={{m|ine-pro|*we-dʰrom}}), from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-||to blow}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|wather|t=weather}}, {{cog|stq|Weeder|t=weather}}, {{cog|cim|bèttar|t=weather}}, {{cog|nl|weder}}, {{m|nl|weer|t=weather}}, {{cog|de|Wetter|t=weather}}, {{cog|nds|Weder|t=weather}}, {{cog|lb|Wieder|t=weather}}, {{cog|yi|וועטער|t=weather}}, {{cog|da|vejr|t=weather}}, {{cog|fo|-}}, {{cog|is|veður|t=weather}}, {{cog|nb|vær|t=weather}}, {{cog|nn|veder}}, {{m|nn|vêr|t=weather}}, {{cog|sv|väder|t=weather}}; also more distantly related to {{cog|ru|вёдро|t=fair weather}} and perhaps {{cog|sq|vrëndë|t=light rain}}.

Other cognates include {{cog|sa|निर्वाण|t=blown or put out, extinguished}}.
